  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1250 PM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA moist southwesterly flow aloft will become more westerly and \nundergo some drying heading into early next week. This drying will\nbring increased breeziness and lowering minimum relative humidity\nvalues. Daily scattered shower and thunderstorm activity will \ncontinue through mid-week, most widespread during a shortwave \npassage late Monday into Tuesday.\n\nRidging aloft strengthens over the Great Basin with heights \nrising as high as the 588 to 594 dm range over the Northern \nRockies by Friday. This will reduce shower and storm coverage and \npush afternoon temperatures back to the upper 80s to near 90 while\nseasonal breeziness continues.\n\nA Pacific trough approaches the region sometime next weekend and \nbrings increased shower and thunderstorm activity with the warmest\ntemperatures of week looking to occur just ahead of this system. \nThis will be followed by breezy to windy conditions and slightly \ncooler temperatures. - RCG \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nDaily rounds of showers and thunderstorms through Tuesday...\n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms are already developing over S\nID with W MT not far behind. This activity will move toward the \nnortheast into central and southwest MT and interact with peak ML \nCAPE generally running between 500 and 1000 J/kg and effective \nshear above 20 kts. With surfaces beginning to dry out, localized \nstrong, gusty winds, lightning, and perhaps some small hail will \nbe the primary hazards, but PWATS as high as 0.80 inches may bring\nsome heavier downpours capable of localized flooding, \nparticularly for the more sensitive burn scars. No flood products \nare planned for today given the isolated nature of the heavier \ndownpours, but conditions will be closely monitored through the \nevening hours. Showers and storms will weaken in coverage and \nintensity as they move northward into north-central MT later this \nevening into the overnight hours.\n\nWesterly flow aloft will begin to thin the moisture profile \nSunday and Monday, but will maintain at least similar shear and \ninstability for continued scattered shower and thunderstorm \nactivity. Dryer sub- cloud layers will increase instances of \nstrong to severe wind gusts while decreasing the localized \nflooding threat. All locations will be subjective stronger storms,\nalthough southwest MT and areas along the Hi-line will be most \nsusceptible. Showers and storms will be most widespread during a \nshortwave passage late Monday into Tuesday, although the timing of\nthe trough and attendant cold front will determine whether this \nactivity will be able to take advantage of the peak diurnal \ninstability for stronger storm initiation. - RCG",
